window.docContents[160].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Definitions":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §4.28, Definitions.\n\n\\sinh z=\\frac{e^z-e^{-z}}{2}.Hyperbolic sine is the odd part of the exponential, with z allowed to be\nany complex number.\n\n\\cosh z=\\frac{e^z+e^{-z}}{2}.Hyperbolic cosine is the even part of the exponential, on the same entire\ncomplex domain.\n\n\\cosh z\\mathbin{\\pm}\\sinh z=e^{\\pm z}.The two signs are recorded together: the plus combination is exp z, and\nthe minus combination is exp (-z).\n\n\\tanh z=\\frac{\\sinh z}{\\cosh z}.The hyperbolic tangent is the quotient of hyperbolic sine by hyperbolic\ncosine.  Its poles occur where the denominator vanishes.\n\n\\operatorname{csch} z=\\frac1{\\sinh z}.The hyperbolic cosecant is the reciprocal of hyperbolic sine, with the source\ndomain understood away from the zeros of sinh.\n\n\\operatorname{sech} z=\\frac1{\\cosh z}.The hyperbolic secant is the reciprocal of hyperbolic cosine, away from its\nzero set.\n\n\\coth z=\\frac1{\\tanh z}.The hyperbolic cotangent is the reciprocal of hyperbolic tangent, with the\ncorresponding pole convention.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity","header":"Definitions","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Definitions"}});